layout "layout.faqir"

block content
  p
    | This page is for whoever looks after the Buildyboy server: installing it, updating it, keeping backups and
    | restoring them. The standard setup is Ubuntu inside WSL on a Windows PC, running Buildyboy as a system service
    | behind the Caddy web server, which provides the https address. The same scripts work on a regular Ubuntu server.

  h2#layout What lives where
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") What
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Where (standard install)
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") The app
          td(data-part="td")
            code ~/apps/buildyboy
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") The previous version, kept after an update
          td(data-part="td")
            code ~/apps/buildyboy.prev
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") The data: database, settings, keys
          td(data-part="td")
            code ~/.local/share/buildyboy
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") Nightly backups
          td(data-part="td")
            code ~/.local/share/buildyboy/backups
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") The service
          td(data-part="td")
            code /etc/systemd/system/buildyboy.service
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") The web address (Caddy site)
          td(data-part="td")
            code /etc/caddy/sites/buildyboy.caddy
  p
    | The service listens only on
    code 127.0.0.1:7700
    | ; Caddy publishes it at
    code {{ site.url }}
    | with an automatic certificate. Everything Buildyboy knows is in one SQLite database file,
    code buildyboy.db
    | , in the data folder.

  h2#install Installing for the first time
  p Inside WSL (or on the server), you need systemd switched on, Node.js 24 installed with nvm, rsync, curl, Caddy, and sudo rights. Then, from a copy of the code:
  pre
    code
      | DOMAIN={{ site.host }} deploy/deploy-wsl.sh --dry-run   # shows every step, changes nothing
      | DOMAIN={{ site.host }} deploy/deploy-wsl.sh             # does it
  p The script prints each command before running it, and can be run again safely. It goes through six steps:
  ol
    li
      strong Preflight
      | : checks Node 24, the port, Caddy, and that the existing Caddy configuration is valid.
    li
      strong Install
      | : copies the app to
      code ~/apps/buildyboy
      | , installs its dependencies and builds the web app.
    li
      strong Service
      | : installs and enables the
      code buildyboy
      | systemd service, locked down to its own data folder.
    li
      strong Caddy
      | : adds a site file for your domain and reloads Caddy. If anything fails it puts the old configuration back. Other
      | sites on the same Caddy are never edited.
    li
      strong Backups
      | : installs a timer that backs the database up every night.
    li
      strong Finish
      | : waits until
      code /api/health
      | answers and prints the one-time setup link for your password.
  +callout(variant="warning")
    @content
      p
        strong Always pass your DOMAIN.
        | Without it the script uses its built-in default address and rewrites the Caddy site and the service's public
        | address to it, which breaks installer commands, notification links and the setup link. For this server that
        | means
        code DOMAIN={{ site.host }}
        | .
  p
    | Your domain needs a DNS record pointing at the machine's public address, and ports 80 and 443 must reach Caddy
    | (on a home PC: forwarded by the router to the PC, and from Windows into WSL).

  h2#update Updating
  p
    | To install a newer version, run the update script from a copy of the code (not from
    code ~/apps/buildyboy
    | itself):
  pre
    code
      | deploy/update.sh --dry-run    # see what it would do
      | deploy/update.sh              # pull, back up, build, swap, restart, check
  p It is careful by design:
  ol
    li pulls the latest code with git (skip with --no-pull),
    li backs the database up,
    li builds the new version next to the running one,
    li swaps them, keeping the old one as buildyboy.prev, and restarts the service,
    li
      | waits up to 45 seconds for
      code /api/health
      | ; if the new version does not come up healthy, it puts the previous version back by itself.
  p
    | It does not touch Caddy or the service settings, so it is the safe way to ship code changes. Database changes run
    | automatically when the new version starts and only go forward; the backup taken before the update is your way
    | back.

  h2#status Checking on it
  pre
    code
      | systemctl status buildyboy             # is it running?
      | journalctl -u buildyboy -n 80          # the last 80 log lines
      | journalctl -u buildyboy -f             # follow the log live
      | curl -s http://127.0.0.1:7700/api/health
  p
    code /api/health
    | answers with
    code {"ok":true,…}
    | when the server is up. Restarting needs sudo:
    code sudo systemctl restart buildyboy
    | .

  h2#backups Backups
  p
    | A timer backs the database up every night at about 03:30 into the
    code backups
    | folder, and keeps the newest 14. Backups are made while the server runs, without stopping it. To make one now:
  pre
    code
      | cd ~/apps/buildyboy && BUILDYBOY_DATA=~/.local/share/buildyboy node cli/bb.ts backup
  p
    | Backups live on the same disk as the database. For real safety, copy the backups folder somewhere else from time
    | to time, for example to Windows (
    code /mnt/c/…
    | ) or to another machine.
  h3#restore Restoring a backup
  ol
    li
      | Stop the server:
      code sudo systemctl stop buildyboy
    li
      | Keep a copy of the current database, copy the backup you want over it, and remove the two files next to it that
      | end in
      code -wal
      | and
      code -shm
      | :
      pre
        code
          | cd ~/.local/share/buildyboy
          | cp buildyboy.db buildyboy.db.before-restore
          | cp backups/buildyboy-2026-10-17T03-30-12-000Z.db buildyboy.db
          | rm -f buildyboy.db-wal buildyboy.db-shm
    li
      | Start it again with
      code sudo systemctl start buildyboy
      | and check the log.
  p
    | Restore a backup with the same version of Buildyboy that made it, or a newer one: a newer version upgrades an
    | older database when it starts, but an older version cannot open a database a newer one has changed.

  h2#settings Server settings
  p The service reads these environment variables (set in its systemd unit):
  div(data-ui="table")
    table(data-part="table")
      thead(data-part="thead")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Variable
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Default
          th(data-part="th" scope="col") Meaning
      tbody(data-part="tbody")
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") BUILDYBOY_PUBLIC_URL
          td(data-part="td") http://127.0.0.1:7700
          td(data-part="td") The address people and machines use. It appears in installer commands, links and notifications.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") BUILDYBOY_PORT
          td(data-part="td") 7700
          td(data-part="td") The port to listen on.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") BUILDYBOY_HOST
          td(data-part="td") 127.0.0.1
          td(data-part="td") The address to listen on. Keep it local behind Caddy.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") BUILDYBOY_DATA
          td(data-part="td") ./data
          td(data-part="td") The data folder.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") BUILDYBOY_PERMISSION_WAIT_MS
          td(data-part="td") 570000 (9½ min)
          td(data-part="td") How long a walk-mode permission, question or plan waits for an answer.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") BUILDYBOY_STOP_WAIT_MIN
          td(data-part="td") 5
          td(data-part="td") The default end-of-turn wait in walk mode, in minutes (Settings can override it).
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") BUILDYBOY_PAUSE_WAIT_MS
          td(data-part="td") 570000 (9½ min)
          td(data-part="td") The longest a paused session is held per tool call.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") BUILDYBOY_INITIAL_PASSWORD
          td(data-part="td") none
          td(data-part="td") Sets the password on start if none exists yet. Handy for automated setups; remove it afterwards.
        tr(data-part="tr")
          td(data-part="td") NODE_ENV
          td(data-part="td") production on the service
          td(data-part="td") Production mode.

  h2#uninstall Uninstalling
  pre
    code
      | deploy/deploy-wsl.sh --uninstall
  p
    | This removes the service, the backup timer and the Caddy site, and reloads Caddy. It leaves the app folder and the
    | data folder, so nothing is lost until you delete them yourself.

  h2#local Running it on your own computer
  p To try Buildyboy or work on it, run it locally with Node.js 24:
  pre
    code
      | npm ci
      | npm run dev        # the server on :7700 and the web app on :5173
      | npm run simulate   # fill the Floor with three made-up sessions
  p
    | Open
    code http://localhost:5173
    | . The first start prints the setup link in the terminal.
